{
  "statement": "The document's central claim is that lower AI adoption in women-led firms reflects unequal access to capital and tools built around other users, not a knowledge gap that training can fix. That claim is a hypothesis, not a finding. The document supplies no adoption, funding, or vendor data. No remedy should be funded or released until a binding, matched-firm measurement shows which cause is at work and whether the tools work for the firms they reach.\n\nThe Skeptic's position deserves its strongest form. A raw gap cannot show a skills problem, because women-led firms skew small, young, and service-oriented, and those firms adopt less whoever runs them. A matched-firm comparison should decide the question, and some firms may decline tools that fail them. That reasoning is sound. The Accelerationist's case is also strong. A firm priced out of useful tools pays a cost that compounds, and cheaper entry through free tiers, new vendors, and open model weights would widen the seller pool faster than any reporting rule. Both speakers want the same matched-firm test. Neither says who must supply the data or what happens if vendors and lenders refuse. Both positions can hold only if the test actually runs, and a test that depends on goodwill will not.\n\nCompelled disclosure is the precedent. Section 1071 of Dodd-Frank, enacted in 2010, ordered lenders to report small-business credit by owner sex. The final rule took until 2023. The Equal Credit Opportunity Act of 1974 and the Census Annual Business Survey show the same pattern: lending disparities became measurable only once disclosure was required. Apply that here. Vendors selling AI tools to small firms should report adoption and pricing tiers by owner sex, firm size, sector, and age. The first phase can be voluntary, but the law should set a dated switch to mandatory reporting. Voluntary standards often harden into permanent substitutes for rules, and that risk is real. Thresholds by vendor size and a short list of fields would spare new entrants the compliance burden that favors incumbents. Program spending should wait on the result. If the gap persists after matching, structure is the cause. If it vanishes, composition explains it.\n\nCheaper entry is a fair goal, but open weights are the wrong means. Once a model's weights are released, no one can recall, patch, or shut it down. The source documents no harm to small buyers from open release, and this argument does not claim one. The claim is narrower. A price remedy should not depend on a step that cannot be undone when audited hosted access can do the job. Subsidized tiers, public pricing terms, and data-retention limits in hosted contracts all lower cost and keep a named party answerable. Concentrated vendor control has its own failure modes, including lock-in, which is why the terms must be public and audited. A cash-poor buyer also cannot check what a tool does with business data. That is a present limit, not a permanent one, since interpretability research may narrow it. Competition alone will not close the gap either. Vendors compete on price and speed, not on testing products across varied customers.\n\nThe phrase \"infrastructure built around other users\" is a testable claim about product design, so test it. Vendors should run pre-release trials on varied customer groups and publish the results. The Brooks 2014 study offers the method: 68 percent of investors preferred the same pitch when a male voice delivered it, which isolated structure by holding content fixed. That study concerns funding, not AI, and no data in the source show these tools perform worse for women-led firms. Treat it as an open question. Capital versus knowledge is also a false split. The Sri Lanka grant trials found that cash alone did not lift women-owned firms, and training reviews show modest gains. A firm needs money to buy, skill to integrate, and the ability to check what it bought. Training built to raise use rates can teach reliance without verification. Judge any program by firm outcomes and error-checking skill, not by adoption counts. Adoption is a means. If it is pushed before anyone can show that the tools are reliable for these firms, Congress will have paid for the gap to be filled with products no one has tested on them."
  ,
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "📏", "tooltip": "Like surveying land before building, the data must show the cause of the gap before anyone pays for a remedy."},
    {"symbol": "🔌", "tooltip": "Cheaper entry is welcome, but a power cord must still lead to a socket someone can switch off."},
    {"symbol": "🧪", "tooltip": "A paired-pitch test isolates structure by changing one variable, and vendor trials on varied customers would do the same for tools."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The cause of the gap is unproven, so no remedy should be funded or released until a binding, matched-firm measurement shows which cause is at work.", "targets": ["AN-4"], "relationship": "extends"},
    {"claim": "Both speakers want the same matched-firm test. Neither says who must supply the data or what happens if vendors and lenders refuse.", "targets": ["AN-4", "AN-5"], "relationship": "integrates"},
    {"claim": "Vendors selling AI tools to small firms should report adoption and pricing tiers by owner sex, firm size, sector, and age, with a dated switch to mandatory reporting.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Cheaper entry is a fair goal, but open weights are the wrong means, because once weights are released no one can recall, patch, or shut the model down.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A price remedy should not depend on a step that cannot be undone when audited hosted access can do the job.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Vendors should run pre-release trials on varied customer groups and publish the results.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Training built to raise use rates can teach reliance without verification, so programs should be judged by firm outcomes, not adoption counts.", "targets": ["AN-5"], "relationship": "specifies"}
  ],
  "prior_steelmans": [
    {"speaker": "Skeptic", "steelman_claim": "A raw adoption gap cannot show a skills problem because women-led firms skew small, young, and service-oriented, so a matched-firm test must decide the cause, and some firms may rationally decline tools that fail them."},
    {"speaker": "Accelerationist", "steelman_claim": "Firms priced out of useful AI tools pay a cost that compounds, so cheaper entry through open weights, free tiers, and new vendors, with training built into the tools, will change vendor design faster than a reporting rule."}
  ]
}

