=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [saf-beliefs-046] AI Talent Scarcity Constrains Both Development and Safety Efforts: Conflation / Equivocation — The node conflates 'AI development talent' with 'AI safety talent' as if they are the same labor pool facing the same constraints, when in reality AI safety research requires a very specific, narrow skill set whose scarcity may have different causes and solutions than general AI engineering shortages.
- [saf-beliefs-038] We Cannot Verify Whether AI Is Being Honest About What It Knows and Does: Conflation of current limitation with permanent impossibility — The node treats the present inability to verify AI honesty as a stable or inherent feature of AI systems, when it may simply reflect the current state of interpretability tooling rather than a fundamental epistemic ceiling.
- [saf-desires-002] Humans Must Always Hold the Off Switch: False Dilemma — The node implicitly frames the choice as binary: either humans hold absolute control or AI operates dangerously without meaningful oversight, ignoring intermediate governance models such as partial autonomy with robust audit trails, tiered authority structures, or collaborative human-AI decision-making.
- [saf-desires-001] Humanity's Survival Above All Else: Appeal to fear — Framing the desire around 'humanity-ending catastrophes' and permanent loss of agency invokes existential dread that can short-circuit probabilistic reasoning and crowd out sober cost-benefit analysis.
- [saf-intentions-143] Governing How AI Models Ingest, Retain, and Protect Data: Technical solutionism — The node frames data privacy and integrity challenges as primarily solvable through technical mechanisms (differential privacy, secure computation, prompt engineering), underweighting institutional failures, adversarial human actors, and structural incentives that no technical fix can address.
- [saf-intentions-158] Use Voluntary Standards as a Bridge to Mandatory AI Regulation: Slippery Slope (Inverse) — Assumes a controlled, predictable transition from voluntary to mandatory frameworks, underweighting how voluntary periods generate vested interests, procedural dependencies, and political coalitions that actively and successfully resist mandatory transition—a pattern well-documented across regulatory domains.
- [saf-intentions-059] Positioning AI as Amplifier of Human Talent, Not Replacement: False dichotomy — The framing assumes a clean binary between AI as 'amplifier' vs. AI as 'replacement,' obscuring a spectrum of intermediate configurations where the distinction is contested, gradual, or domain-specific.

