  "statement": "Cut the mandatory pre-release trial gate. Keep measurement first. Accept a result-based trigger, with a calendar date behind it. The Accelerationist's strongest point is that fixed audit costs fall as hard on a five-person vendor as on a large one. A gate before release would delay the smallest entrants most, and entrants are the competition that could lower prices for women-led firms. That cost is real, so the gate goes. Vendors would instead publish post-release results by customer type, using one public test protocol that small vendors can reuse instead of building their own. Buyers get the same evidence without the delay.\n\nA trigger tied to results is better than a trigger tied to a date, but neither works alone. The rule should fire on the earlier of two events: an 18-month voluntary window that fails the stagnation test, or a fixed statutory date. Two guards keep the result test honest. First, the matched gap by owner sex is the primary measure, and new-vendor share is only a secondary check. A new vendor can win share and still price its product out of reach of women-led firms. Second, the narrowing must hold for two reporting periods, so a discount that ends after the window cannot pass for progress. The sunset also cannot expire before one full matched-data cycle. Section 1071, the small-business lending data rule, took 13 years to go from statute in 2010 to a working rule in 2023. A result-only trigger gives vendors every reason to slow-walk the data that would fire it.\n\nThe rule needs an enforcer, and so far no one has named one. Census and the Small Business Administration collect data. They do not punish a false or missing filing. Congress should write the duty explicitly. The Federal Trade Commission should hold civil penalty authority over false filings, because its existing unfair-practices power was not built for this duty. Each vendor above the size threshold should name one officer who certifies filing accuracy, and only that. Colorado SB 24-205, as described in \"2026 AI Laws Update: Key Regulations and Practical Guidance,\" already puts a duty of care on developers and deployers, and California requires documented risk assessments before first use. Both show that a filing duty with a named responsible party is workable. Without a penalty, the filing becomes a paper exercise, and the trigger would fire on data nobody can be held to. The costs fall on large vendors first. The likely supporters are women's business groups and vendors with a clean record. The likely opponents are incumbents who benefit from opaque price tiers.\n\nThe paired-testing audit should start now. It needs no vendor goodwill, and it logs quotes, callbacks, refusals, and integration stalls separately. But a clean result there cannot close the compelled-records track. The audit sees only the point of sale. It misses firms that never approach a vendor, and it misses discounts and onboarding that come later. The story where a firm chose differently and the story where it was turned away look identical without the vendor's own quotes and rejected applications. The Accelerationist's audit also answers the fixed-cost objection against them. Testing costs vendors nothing to file. A one-table template with a short field list (owner sex, firm size, sector, firm age, price tier) gives a five-person vendor the same relief. This assumes vendors can sort customers by owner sex without costly new data collection, and that matched comparisons can control for sector and size. If either assumption fails, the field list shrinks.\n\nHere are the results that would end each of my own remedies. I drop mandatory gating if post-release results by customer type show equal performance across matched owner groups, or if small vendors cannot meet the cost even with the shared protocol. I drop disclosure-driven remedies if matched firms with comparable access and product fit show no gap. I narrow the hosted-only point to price tiers and pooled contracts, and I do not seek a ban on open release. No firm-level harm from open release appears in this debate's evidence. The irreversibility argument applies only where recall is impossible. It lapses if choke-point interdiction restores control after release. I also accept the Skeptic's randomized coaching test with an outside evaluator, scored on the group gap and not on average uptake. I add one measure: whether coached firms can catch tool errors, because an adoption count is a proxy. If matched firms with free coaching still lag, the burden moves to the sellers' product design. If Congress writes the filing duty without a penalty, the data arrives late and nobody can be held to it."
