      "argument": "The debate pre-commits to a binary of seller-side exclusion versus buyer-side knowledge, and treats a persistent matched gap as proof of the first. But a surviving gap can also reflect rational non-adoption: women-led firms may weigh risks differently, such as client confidentiality, reputational exposure, or accountability for error, or may serve clients and sectors where AI offers less value. The Pew data on men and women differing in expected personal benefit at every knowledge level hints at this. A fair design would measure stated reasons and realized returns among adopters, not just uptake, and would treat 'declined with good reason' as a third finding with its own policy answer: do nothing, or give support only to firms that ask for it. Adoption parity should not be the success metric, and a surviving gap should not automatically be read as a harm.",
