{
  "statement": "The coaching test would count against the structural story, and the story should be willing to lose to it. Suppose firms with free, self-teaching, coaching-equipped tools reach parity with male-led firms of the same size, sector, and age. Then knowledge was the main gap, and the exclusion argument fails. That is the Accelerationist's best point: capital and know-how may be a sequencing problem, and a good tool can supply the know-how. But the test only works if it measures the right thing. Coaching can lift both groups and leave the distance between them untouched. A headline like \"adoption rose 20%\" says nothing about the gap. Averages hide exactly that. The pilot has to compare matched groups, be pre-registered, and be graded by an outside evaluator. Vendor-run coaching is also a sales channel, so the vendor cannot grade its own homework.\n\nThe Accelerationist's own remedies already treat the barrier as structural. They point to antitrust action on tying and exclusive channel deals, open-interface rules in federal purchasing, and the 1974 Equal Credit Opportunity Act (ECOA) as the model. ECOA put the burden on lenders, not on borrowers taking classes. A rule on sellers and lenders is a rule about their conduct. Here is the uncomfortable part for my side: no statute bans sex-based pricing or channel design in software sales. ECOA's reach on disparate impact is also still disputed. So the first step is disclosure, not prohibition. Securities law shows the path. The SEC does not tell companies what to earn. It makes them report in a standard form so outsiders can check. Vendors above a size threshold would report adoption and price tiers by owner sex, firm size, sector, and age. That threshold protects small vendors from the compliance burden. Census and the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) already hold firm and lender data, including Section 1071 small-business lending data, whose rule took until 2023. The FTC and DOJ hold the antitrust and unfair-practices tools. Taxpayers fund Census. Large vendors pay for reporting. Incumbent vendors will oppose it, and small-business groups will back it.\n\nOn more sellers, open release is necessary and not sufficient. Free entry should discipline intermediaries in theory, and sometimes it does. But a small-business owner does not lack access to model weights. They lack an integrator, a sales channel that calls them back, and a vendor they trust with payroll data. The Accelerationist admits open weights do not pay for integration. That admission is the whole problem, because entry fails at integration, channels, and trust. The Safetyist's ban on open weights fails on the same logic. It removes a cheap input and fixes nothing downstream. A third path is pooled buying. SBA-linked women's business centers could negotiate group contracts, which gives small buyers the bargaining power they lack against take-it-or-leave-it price tiers. Its failure condition is stagnation: a pooled buyer can lock in last year's product and starve new entrants. If pooled contracts raise adoption but entrants do not win share, drop them.\n\nSri Lanka cuts both ways, and the result should be read plainly. Cash grants alone did not lift women-owned microenterprises. That undercuts a pure capital story and undercuts cheaper entry as a sufficient fix. It points to product fit and channels, which is what structure means here. But the study covers microenterprises and money, not AI, so it is suggestive only. The same caution applies to attitude surveys. Pew's \"pi 2025.04.03 us public and ai experts report\" finds that 18% of women versus 31% of men foresee personal benefit from AI. That number records what people say. It cannot show whether a vendor turned them away, priced them out, or whether they chose differently. Only transaction records can answer that.\n\nTwo assumptions carry my argument. First, vendor behavior differs by owner sex after controls, which is unproven. Second, firm-level data will show a gap that survives matching. Run that first test now, with no mandate. Census can do it on existing survey data, and the Accelerationist is right that waiting costs cash-poor firms. The vendor-side question cannot be run that way, because no one holds the records. So the Accelerationist's own test has a hole. If Census runs the firm comparison and vendors keep their sales and pricing data private, who finds out whether women-led firms were refused, priced out, or simply chose differently?",
