  "statement": "I conditionally agree: structural barriers are the better-supported hypothesis, and I concede the dated vendor-disclosure mandate, the dropped pre-release gate, and that disclosure alone fixes nothing. I still hold that quotes, refusals, and product fit explain the gap better than an outreach deficit that training could close. I would change my view if a matched-firm gap, with a big enough sample and a split by purchasing authority, stays at zero for two reporting periods with equal quotes and refusals.\n\nThe evidence on hand is thinner than anyone's confidence. Pew's 31 versus 18 percent measures opinion among adults, not firm adoption. The Sri Lanka training result covers microenterprises, not AI. Census data codes who owns a firm, not who picks the tools. So owner sex may be the wrong unit. The person who holds buying and configuration authority may matter more, and a matched-firm null could be a false negative if that person is mixed in with the owner. That is why the split by purchasing authority is a condition of my position, not a footnote.\n\nThe concessions cost something. Disclosure finds the cause and fixes nothing, so product-fit trials and pooled buying still have to do the fixing. The Safetyist dropped the pre-release gate, and I never asked for it. A five-person vendor cannot carry a fixed audit bill, and those are the entrants who might cut prices. The Accelerationist's symmetric kill conditions stand: every remedy gets a stated exit. One refinement is needed. The calendar date should fire the filing duty only, and remedies stay gated on the result. A vendor who can stall a result cannot stall a date, and the Section 1071 delay from 2010 to 2023 is a warning, though rulemaking politics may explain part of it. Reporting without a penalty is a paper trail. \"Auditing large language models for race & gender disparities\" notes that New York's Local Law 144 produced only 19 published audits in its first six months. The FTC civil-penalty power and a named certifying officer are the minimum. \"The AI governance mirage\" finds 72 percent of enterprises lack the control they think they have. Self-certification by vendors repeats that error.\n\nMy kill conditions are specific. First, the mandate drops only if the matched gap is statistically indistinguishable from zero for two periods and audits show equal quotes, callbacks, and refusals. The test must also be pre-registered with a minimum detectable gap. An underpowered null counts as inconclusive, not a repeal, and the fix for thin cells is a larger sample, not a looser test. Second, pooled buying fails if uptake stalls or one mediocre tool locks in. Third, open weights are necessary but not sufficient. They do not pay for integration, open sales channels, or earn trust. I would neither ban them nor freeze program spending. Fourth, a hosted-only price remedy should lapse once irreversibility is no longer the issue. \"Declined for good reason\" is a fair outcome. It becomes a vendor escape hatch unless stated reasons are checked against quote and refusal records and returns are scored among adopters. Independent evaluators, as \"Outsmarting Smart Devices\" argues, keep that check honest.\n\nThe Accelerationist and I still disagree on one question. Who holds the vendor records, and who pays if they do not exist? I say missing records count against the vendor, because a result-only trigger run on records the vendor controls can be slow-walked. The Safetyist and I disagree on the frame. A hosted-only price remedy and an owner-sex table assume the gap sits at the point of sale. That misses firms that never approach a vendor. Who pays if the gap persists after free coaching? If records show unequal quotes or refusals, vendors pay through the filing duty, penalties, and product-fit duties. If records show equal treatment, neither owners nor vendors owe anything, and the fix is publicly funded or it is nothing. Either way, the onus does not land on women owners, which is the deficit framing the skeptics reject. So if the records show equal treatment and the gap is real, who writes that check, and what happens when no one will?",
